Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces and fabric; candlelight, deep shadows, 1840s interiors.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

A narrow boarded room. Shutters close-fastened. An OLD MAN (seventies, thin white hair, sunken cheeks) sleeps beneath a heavy coverlet. A pale blue eye shows a dull film even in rest. Beside the bed, a dark lantern sits closed.

(SOUND: distant death-watch beetles ticking in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

Candle smoke drifts. Floorboards, a wash-tub in the corner, three loose planks near the hearth.

(SOUND: a single floorboard creaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR (thirties, lean, pale, dark waistcoat, shirt sleeves rolled, eyes too bright) stands at a latched door, lantern in hand. He listens, smiling thinly.

(SOUND: his breath, slow and measured; house settling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

He turns the latch with two fingers—oh, so gently.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The door opens a hand's width. NARRATOR slides the closed lantern in, then his head, inch by inch.

(SOUND: hinge whispers; old man's soft snore)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

A thin ray cracks from the lantern and finds the sleeping face—never the open eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me.

INT. SITTING ROOM - DAY

Morning light through dusty glass. NARRATOR sets a cup beside the OLD MAN, who sits wrapped in a shawl, the filmed blue eye half-lidded.

(SOUND: spoon against china; street cart distant)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN nods, mild, unsuspecting. NARRATOR's smile is hearty, too hearty.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Again midnight. NARRATOR's head in the opening. The ray seeks the pillow. The eye is closed.

(SOUND: watch-tick of beetles; lantern tin faintly rings)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

He withdraws without a sound. Seven nights marked only by the same careful ritual.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

Eighth night. NARRATOR at the door, triumphant, almost laughing under his breath. His hand on the latch moves slower than a watch's minute hand.

(SOUND: his stifled chuckle; latch tongue easing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Pitch black. Shutters sealed. The door creeps inward. On the bed the OLD MAN stirs suddenly.

(SOUND: bed ropes creak; fabric rasp)

NARRATOR freezes in the opening, head in, lantern still shut. His thumb slips on the tin fastening.

(SOUND: sharp tin click)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Silence. NARRATOR does not move a muscle. The OLD MAN sits up in the dark, listening.

(SOUND: death watches in the wall, steady)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

A low, stifled groan—mortal terror, not pain.

(SOUND: the groan, thin and continuous under the ticking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom.

NARRATOR's eye gleams. He pities and chuckles at once, soundless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been saying to himself—it is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.

He opens a crevice in the lantern. A single dim ray, like a spider's thread, falls full upon the vulture eye.

(SOUND: tiny hinge creak of the lantern)

The eye is open—wide, wide open—dull blue under its hideous veil. Nothing else of the face is lit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick pulse—as a watch muffled in cotton)

The beat grows. NARRATOR holds the ray steady, scarcely breathing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

Louder. Quicker. NARRATOR's face tightens with fury and terror.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder, faster; house otherwise dead silent)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I have told you that I am nervous: so I am.

He shakes. The beat seems fit to burst—and to carry through the wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell he throws the lantern wide and leaps in. The OLD MAN shrieks once—once only.

(SOUND: yell; single shriek; bed crashing)

NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him. Then he stands, smiling gaily.

(MUSIC: low tense drone under the muffled beat)

(SOUND: muffled heart still beating beneath the bed)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This, however, did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall.

The beat falters. Stops. NARRATOR heaves the bed aside. He kneels, hand on the still chest, waiting.

(SOUND: silence where the heart was; his own breath harsh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes.

No pulsation. He draws back, eyes bright.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Later. A tub stands ready. NARRATOR works in silence—dismembers the corpse: head, arms, legs. No blood reaches the boards.

(SOUND: wet work muffled; wood scrape; no drip on floor)

He lifts three planks, deposits the parts between the scantlings, replaces the boards flush and clean.

(SOUND: planks set home; soft hammer of palm on wood)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

He wipes nothing—there is nothing to wipe. He laughs once under his breath.

NARRATOR
Ha! ha!

(SOUND: his short laugh; far church bell beginning to toll)

INT. STREET DOOR - NIGHT

Four o'clock. Still dark. The bell finishes. A firm knocking at the street door. NARRATOR descends with a light heart and opens.

(SOUND: iron knocker; bell's last stroke; door latch)

Three officers in coats and badges: OFFICER REYNOLDS (bearded, calm), OFFICER HALE (younger, watchful), OFFICER BRIGGS (broad, polite).

(SOUND: night wind in the street; their boots on the step)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. We are to search the premises.

NARRATOR smiles, bows them in.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen—welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

INT. SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR leads them through, lamps raised. Chests, chairs, ordinary clutter—undisturbed.

(SOUND: boots on boards; lamp glass chiming)

NARRATOR
Search—search well. You will find all in order.

OFFICER HALE exchanges a glance with OFFICER BRIGGS; nothing amiss.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR shows the bed, the treasures secure. In wild confidence he carries in chairs. He sets his own seat on the very boards above the corpse.

(SOUND: chair legs planted on the hollow spot)

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues. I am singularly at ease.

The officers sit. They chat of familiar things. NARRATOR answers cheerily—then pales.

(SOUND: pleasant low conversation; a faint ringing rising under it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

The ringing sharpens—not in the ear alone. A low, dull, quick sound: a watch in cotton.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat returning, soft at first)

NARRATOR talks more freely, voice climbing. The officers still smile, still chat.

NARRATOR
A fine night for the watch, is it not? Quite fine—yes—

(SOUND: heartbeat louder; his chair scrapes)

He gasps, then argues about trifles, high and vehement, hands cutting the air.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. Why would they not be gone?

He paces heavy strides. Foams. Raves. Swings his chair and grates it on the boards.

(SOUND: chair wood screaming on planks; heartbeat over all—louder, louder)

(MUSIC: dissonant pulse matching the beat)

OFFICER REYNOLDS and the others chat on, pleasant, smiling. NARRATOR stares, breaking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!

He cannot bear the smiles. The beat is agony—louder—louder—louder—

(SOUND: heartbeat overwhelming; his ragged scream building)

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!

He stabs a finger at the floor beneath his chair.

(SOUND: his shriek echoing off the shutters)

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers rise. REYNOLDS looks at the boards. The beat pounds on in NARRATOR's face alone.

(SOUND: heartbeat thundering; then cut to bare room tone)

FADE OUT.

THE END
